# Generated / vendored trees. Formatting these produces churn that is
# regenerated on every build and only obscures real diffs.
node_modules/
.next/
out/
build/
coverage/
tool-results/

# Vendored minified bundles are downloaded artifacts, not source. Reformatting
# one expands it into a diff nobody can review, and the next download of the
# same file undoes it.
**/*.min.js
**/*.min.css

# Prisma ORM v7 client output: regenerable TypeScript that lives under src/
# so the bundler can compile it. Same category as tool-results/.
src/generated/

# Evidence artifacts are machine-written JSON. Prettier would reformat them
# into a shape the producing script does not emit, so every regeneration
# would show up as a spurious diff and the artifact would stop being
# byte-comparable against the run that produced it.
evidence/

# Lockfiles and generated manifests.
bun.lockb
bun.lock
package-lock.json
pnpm-lock.yaml
yarn.lock

# Caddyfile and Compose are not JS/TS; Prettier has no dialect for them and
# guessing would reformat working infrastructure config.
Caddyfile
Caddyfile.*
docker-compose*.yml
docker-compose*.yaml
Dockerfile*

# GitNexus writes its code-intelligence cache into .gitnexus/ and regenerates it
# on every analyze. It is machine output, excluded from git via .git/info/exclude,
# and reformatting it would be rewritten by the next index run - so the format
# gate would fail on a file no human owns.
.gitnexus/

# Kilo Code (an external agent IDE) writes its planning scratch into .kilo/.
# Same category as .gitnexus/: machine-written, no human hand-edits it, and
# letting it redden the format gate would block the repo on a foreign tool's
# output.
.kilo/

# Agent-instruction files emitted by `next dev`. Next writes them itself
# (node_modules/next/dist/server/lib/generate-agent-files.js) and RE-ADDS them
# on the next dev run, so reformatting them produces a diff that comes straight
# back - and would fight the block that `next dev` maintains inside them. Same
# category as the generated trees above: no human hand-edits these, so no human
# formatting rule should apply.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
# Policy documents the agent cites, one per spoken language. These are compliance
# source text: prettier reflowing a clause changes what a translator and a regulator
# read, and the section markers the coverage gate counts are prose.
agent/policy/**/*.md

# Narration sidecars for the walkthrough demo. `timings.json` is written by
# scripts/walkthrough/narrate.mjs and the copy under source/assets/ is the frozen
# output of the take that produced the .wav files next to it. Reformatting either
# would make the next narrate run look like a content change when it is only a
# whitespace change — the same reason `evidence/` is here. The .wav files and the
# schedule that names them belong to that take, so the whole directory is excluded
# rather than picked file by file.
demo/walkthrough/source/assets/**

# The generated webfont sheet. `scripts/vendor-fonts.mjs` copies each @font-face
# block verbatim out of a real build's CSS, and that CSS is minified — one
# declaration run per rule. Prettier reflowing it would leave the committed file
# no longer byte-equal to what the generator emits, so every refresh would show a
# whole-file diff, and the format gate would fail on bytes nobody hand-wrote.
# Same category as `evidence/`: machine-written, regenerated by a script, and
# reviewed by diffing `public/fonts/manifest.json`, not the sheet.
src/app/fonts.css
